    "updated_at": "2019-08-16T20:44:07Z",
    "body": "To change the terminal title, you need to echo a particular [escape sequence](http://tldp.org/HOWTO/Xterm-Title-3.html). It doesn't matter if it's in the prompt or not--as soon as you echo this, the window title changes.\r\n\r\nThe problem is that starship overwrites both `PROMPT_COMMAND` and `PS1` on initialization, so there's nowhere left for the user to echo this string to the terminal.\r\n\r\nFor `zsh`, the solution is:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# in .zshrc\r\nfunction set_term_title(){\r\n  echo -ne \"\\033]0;${USER}@${HOST} ${PWD}\\007\";\r\n}\r\nprecmd_functions+=(set_term_title)\r\n```\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, there's currently no way to do this in `bash` without a little help from `starship`. I've made a one-line patch that lets you do the following in `bash`:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\n# in .bashrc\r\nfunction set_term_title(){\r\n  echo -ne \"\\033]0;${USER}@${HOST} ${PWD}\\007\";\r\n}\r\nstarship_precmd_user_function=\"set_term_title\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nbut I'll need to do some testing to make sure I haven't broken anything.",
